by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-21T16:46:15Z

Thanks for implementing this @Amoifr, and for deriving the header from the traces instead of touching the cache flow. I reviewed it against RFC 9211 and reworked a few points, pushed on top of your commit.

Behaviour changes:

- non-safe and non-cacheable methods now report `fwd=method` instead of `fwd=bypass`. The RFC asks for the most specific reason known to the cache, and `method` is the one that fits a POST. `bypass` stays for `Expect` and for a store lookup failure.
- `ttl` is computed from `getMaxAge()` and `getAge()` rather than `Response::getTtl()`, which clamps at 0. A stale entry served through stale-while-revalidate now reports a negative `ttl`, which is how the RFC signals staleness.
- `fwd-status` was lost on HEAD requests, because `fetch()` and `validate()` forward them as GET and the status was recorded under the GET trace key. It is now read under that key.
- the cache identifier is quoted when it is not a valid structured-field token, so a value with a space no longer produces an unparsable header.
- no member is added to the 503 that the cache builds on its own when the backend stays locked. The RFC asks caches not to report responses they generate themselves.

Smaller points:

- `fwd-status` and `stored` moved inside the `fwd` branch, since the RFC says both are only meaningful there.
- the `invalidate` and `invalidate-failed` checks were dropped, since `invalidate()` calls `pass()` first and the `pass` branch already matches.
- `addCacheStatus()` moved to the end of the class, after the other private methods. It had landed between `getLog()` and its docblock.

Each of these has a test. I kept `cache_status` rather than `cache_name`: the option turns the header on, and naming it after the value would hide that.

On your question about the default, `null` is the right choice. Turning it on would change the responses of every existing application, and RFC 9211 section 6 is explicit that this header helps an attacker probe the cache. I wrote that reasoning into the description.

The red job on 8.6 is unrelated. It fails on the same two components on the 8.2 base tip.

A symfony-docs pull request is still needed once this lands. The points it should carry are listed at the end of the description.

